**Compiling a c prodecure that doesn't call another procedure**

int leaf_example (int g, int h, int i, int j)
{
  int f;
  f = (g + h) - (i + j);
  return f;
}

assembly instruction explanation
leaf_example:
  addi reserve -12 (corresponding to 12 bytes) on the stack.
  three sw calls to save the current value in the temporary register (x5, x6) and save register (x20) to the stack. Basically borrowing the register.
  perform operations (body of the function)
    two add and one sub stored in x5, x6 and x20 respectively
  addi to copy the value of f into the parameter register
  lw to restore from the stack the old value of the borrowed registers x5, x6, x20
  addi to delete 12 bytes from stack. So x2 now points back to original
  jalr x0, 0(x1) end procedure with a branch register using the return address